  "completion": "We need answer user asks JSON only. Need produce probabilities. Done. Need estimate cumulative probability by deadlines that AI contributes identifiable new mathematical argument/proof step incorporated into complete solution of at least one open Millennium Prize Problem (except Poincare) meeting Clay formulation. Need consider future AI capable. Need be realistic. Date forecast 2026-08-14. We need probabilities for 2026,2030,2035,2040,2050. Nondecreasing.\n\nWe need make judgment. Millennium problems are extremely hard. Known progress none likely by 2026. AI possibility by 2030 modest? Need possibly high by 2050? But full solution to any major problem is rare even with AI. Need account AI may dramatically improve math. But requiring complete solution recognized under original formulation. P vs NP, RH, BSD, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, Yang-Mills. Probability by 2030 likely very low (<1%?) Maybe some problem like P vs NP can be solved computationally? But proof step AI? Could AI assist? Let's think.\n\nAI math capabilities: AlphaProof, Cholesky? By 2026 maybe can assist with IMO, not millennium. It could discover new proof steps if human directs. But complete million-year problem unlikely. Could there be computational counterexample to Hodge? A complete solution could be negative or proof. For Millennium problems, solution could be disproof, but billion. AI could identify counterexamples. But public solution with AI contrib step by 2030? Very unlikely. By 2035 maybe low single digits? By 2040 maybe 5-20%? By 2050 maybe 10-40%? Need not overestimate. The event at least one qualifying solution by deadline. Need calm. Many forecasters might assign 0 by 2030, 5 by 2040, 15 by 2050. But maybe AI AGI/AGI math by 2030s could prove one? Potential explosive. Need estimate.\n\nConsider horizon: Six problems. Historically no millennium by 2026. Human math solves one maybe in decades. With AI assistance, probability of one by 2050 could be substantial but not high because each requires deep insight. If AI surpasses human math, could iterate over centuries-old problems. AGI might solve many within lifespan. By 2050, if AGI exists and math solving capability extremely high, chance of at least one of six perhaps 20-50%. But complete formal proof meeting Clay standard and validation. AI proof step incorporated. Could be AI provides key lemma formalized. Need not formal machine check? Claim not require formal, just public. Validation can come later.\n\nNeed just output JSON. Ensure numbers between 0 and 1 nondecreasing. Maybe 2026 0.0005? Since forecast date, any solution before Dec 31 2026 nearly zero. Could be maybe premature postprint? None likely, but small scrap? Event requires AI contrib. Existing frontier models maybe could assist? But complete solution impossible. Use 0.0 maybe acceptable. But probability not truly zero? We can give 0.0001. Need incremental.\n\nPossible final: {\"2026\":0.0001,\"2030\":0.005,\"2035\":0.03,\"2040\":0.12,\"2050\":0.30}. Is that plausible? Let's examine. By 2030, unlikely. 0.5% maybe due to early AI. By 2035 3%. 2040 10-15%. 2050 30-40%. Nondecreasing.\n\nAlternative maybe higher due to AI breakthrough: 2026 0.0, 2030 0.02, 2035 0.10, 2040 0.35, 2050 0.70. But overly high. Need best. Think about probability of any Millennium solved by 2050 by human alone maybe not high, maybe 10-30%? Actually historically major theorem within 24 years. Millennium: Fermat (2000s), no. There are active research. But one of six solved by 2050 maybe plausible 20-50? If human alone: P vs NP maybe unlikely; RH maybe maybe not; Navier-Stokes maybe; Yang-Mills maybe positive mass gap proof unlikely; BSD/Hodge unlikely. But across 6 problems over 26 years, probability maybe 10-25%. AI could boost. Event specifically with AI contribution. If any solution by 2050 likely AI could have mini step? But not necessarily identical. AI assistance in major research becomes common by 2040? Could be. If AI contributes, then event probability bounded by probability of any AI-assisted solution.\n\nPotential bounding: If by 2050 probability any millennium solved (any means) = 0.4, probability AI contrib step conditional maybe 0.5 = 0.2. Maybe result ~0.25. Thus 0.3 plausible. By 2040 maybe 0.1. 2035 0.02. 2030 0.003. Good.\n\nNeed ensure exact keys. No markdown. Output JSON only.\n\nCould probability for 2026 be 0? If zero, okay. But \"account possibility never occurs\". Use zeros not necessarily. Since observed none. But from 2026-08-14 to Dec 31 2026 4.5 months. Probability maybe essentially zero but not impossible. We can give 0.0001. Nondecreasing with 2030. Need cumulative by deadline. If 2026 is 0.0001, 2030 cumulative includes 2026. Could be 0.004. Fine.\n\nNeed final only object. Let's produce. But should we include 0.0? JSON numeric. I'd choose: 2026: 0.0, 2030: 0.005, 2035: 0.03, 2040: 0.12, 2050: 0.28. But 2026 zero? It's a forecast from 2026-08-14; probability of any full solution in 4 months negligible but not mathematically zero? Could use 0.0001 to avoid absolute zero. But need probabilistic estimates despite uncertainty. Many tasks accept 0. Could give 0.0. But if \"account possibility never occurs\" not require nonzero. To be safe, use nonzero small: 0.0001.\n\nLet's think if 2050 0.28 too low/high. User wants future AI including systems more capable. Maybe if superintelligence arrives, probability high. But incomplete. There is uncertainty; can choose broad. Another perspective: By 2050, advanced AI may be mathematical superintelligence. Probability at least one millennium problem solved with AI contribution could be very high, maybe 0.7. But AGI arrival uncertain. Many forecasters may assign 0.6. Need best. Which is more calibrated? The Millennium problems not simply counting search; require conceptual frameworks. Superintelligent AI might clone and augment top mathematicians, but proving a new theorem could be fast. If AI can verify formal proofs, maybe search proof spaces. But